<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Peloponnesian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 460–445 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Peloponnesian War was fought from about 460 to 445 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and its allies and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, together with Corinth and Thebes. Athens controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> for a time after its victory at Oenophyta (457 BCE) but lost it at Coronea in 447; the Thirty Years' Peace of 446/445 BCE left both alliances intact.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> had fought side by side against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, but Sparta grew wary as Athens rebuilt its walls against Spartan wishes and led the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> from 478 BCE. While the Athenian statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> favoured cooperation, relations held. In 464 BCE an earthquake set off a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helot</a> revolt; Sparta called its allies, and Athens sent 4,000 men under Cimon, but the Spartans, fearing Athenian revolutionary ideas, sent them home alone of all the allies. Cimon was ostracised, and Athens allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and Megara and settled the exiled helots at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Naupactus</a>. By 460 BCE it was at war with Corinth and other Peloponnesian states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> fought on several fronts at once, sending 200 ships to help Inarus's revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. After a defeat at Halieis it won at sea, captured seventy Aeginetan and Peloponnesian ships and besieged Aegina, while an army of the old and the young under Myronides beat the Corinthians at Megara. Holding Megara and its port of Pagae let Athens close the Isthmus. In 458 or 457 BCE a Spartan army under Nicomedes defeated the Athenians at Tanagra, both sides losing heavily, but Athens then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> except Thebes, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locris">Locris</a>, finished its long walls, forced Aegina into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> and sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tolmides">Tolmides</a> to sack the Spartan dockyards.</p><p>In 454 BCE the Egyptian expedition was destroyed after an 18-month siege on Prosopitis, and the league treasury moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Cimon, back from exile in 451 BCE, arranged a five-year truce. Fighting resumed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sacred-war">Second Sacred War</a> over Delphi. After the Boeotian victory at Coronea, Athens gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locris">Locris</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> and Megara revolted, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> persuaded the Spartan king Pleistoanax, perhaps by bribery, to withdraw before crushing Euboea with 50 ships and 5,000 men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under the Thirty Years' Peace Megara returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a>, Troezen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> became independent, Aegina stayed a tributary but autonomous member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>' league, and disputes were to go to arbitration. Athens lost its mainland empire but won Spartan recognition of its alliance, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> two poles. The peace lasted less than half its term: war resumed in 431 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Peloponnesian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
